import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const idx = [
  ["Zampiello", 439, 259, 104, 16, 19, 10, 0],
  ["Annecchino", 603, 63, 215, 237, 40, 0, 9],
];
const avail = [
  ["Arpaia", 99, 61, 61, 43, "Zampiello home town"],
  ["Forchia", 110, 71, 73, 73, "Annecchino home town"],
  ["Moiano", 131, 82, 109, 99, "second cluster of both — unexamined"],
  ["Paolisi", 100, 70, 63, 43, "where Francesco Falco came from in 1714 — unexamined"],
];
---
<Base title="Antenati">
  <h1>What Antenati holds</h1>
  <p class="lede">The Italian State Archives' own portal, which for most of this research was closed to
  us. Opened by hand, it answers two questions that had been left hanging — and the answers are not the
  ones we wanted.</p>

  <h2>Two missing volumes</h2>
  <p>Two people in this archive could not be documented: <strong>Angelo Zampiello</strong>, born at
  Arpaia on 31 August 1895, and <strong>Filomena Annecchino</strong>, born at Forchia on 10 April 1897.
  Neither appears in FamilySearch, and neither appears in Antenati's name index.</p>
  <p>The reason is now clear, and it is the same in both cases. <strong>The volume is not
  digitised.</strong> The archive's own shelf-marks give it away — they run consecutively and skip a
  year:</p>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Arpaia, births</h3>
      <p class="mono small">1890·591 · 1891·592 · 1892·593 · 1893·594 · 1894·595 —
      <strong style="color:var(--accent)">1895 absent</strong> —
      1896·596 · 1897·597 · 1898·598 · 1899·599 · 1900·600</p>
      <p class="small">The shelf-marks do not skip. The year does.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Forchia, births</h3>
      <p class="mono small">1890·3654 · 1891·3655 · 1892·3656 · 1893·3657 · 1894·3658 · 1895·3659 ·
      1896·3660 — <strong style="color:var(--accent)">1897 absent</strong> —
      1898·3661 · 1899·3662 · 1900·3663</p>
      <p class="small">The same pattern, two years and one province apart.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>So these are not research failures. They are <strong>two specific volumes</strong>, each holding one
  of the two births this archive cannot prove, and each sitting un-scanned in the
  <strong>Archivio di Stato di Benevento</strong>. Anyone able to order or read them would close both
  questions at once.</p>

  <h2>Moiano — a cluster nobody had looked at</h2>
  <p>Antenati's name index was harvested in full for both surnames: <strong>439 Zampiello</strong> and
  <strong>603 Annecchino</strong> records. They do not sort the way the FamilySearch harvest suggested.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Surname</th><th>Total</th><th>Arpaia</th><th>Moiano</th><th>Forchia</th><th>S. Agata</th><th>Airola</th><th>Dugenta</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{idx.map(([n,t,a,m,f,s,ai,d]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td class="mono">{t}</td><td class="mono">{a}</td>
        <td class="mono" style="background:var(--bg)"><strong>{m}</strong></td>
        <td class="mono">{f}</td><td class="mono">{s}</td><td class="mono">{ai}</td><td class="mono">{d}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>Moiano is the second stronghold of both families</strong> — 104 Zampiello and 215
  Annecchino — and until now this archive had never once looked there. It sits in the same valley, about
  ten kilometres from Arpaia. Whether the Moiano Zampiello are the same kindred as the Arpaia Zampiello
  is untested; with 37 bearers left in Italy it would be surprising if they were not.</p>

  <h2>The seam that has not been touched</h2>
  <p>Beyond the name index, Antenati holds the register images themselves — and among them the
  <em>processetti</em>, the marriage supplement files. These are the richest genealogical source in the
  Italian system: attached to each marriage are the couple's birth extracts, the parents' consents, and
  the death certificates of any parent already dead. One file can carry two generations backwards.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>Births</th><th>Marriages</th><th>Deaths</th><th>Processetti</th><th></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{avail.map(([t,n,m,d,p,w]) => (
      <tr><td><strong>{t}</strong></td><td class="mono">{n}</td><td class="mono">{m}</td>
        <td class="mono">{d}</td><td class="mono"><strong>{p}</strong></td><td class="small">{w}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>258 volumes of marriage supplements</strong> across these four towns, none of them read.
  That is where Matteo Falco's parentage would be found, if it survives anywhere.</p>


  <h2>Moiano and Paolisi, swept</h2>
  <p>The name index was harvested town by town — 1,087 records.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>Falco</th><th>Zampiello</th><th>Annecchino</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Paolisi</strong></td><td class="mono"><strong>406</strong></td><td class="mono">0</td><td class="mono">1</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Moiano</strong></td><td class="mono"><strong>310</strong></td><td class="mono">104</td><td class="mono">215</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Forchia</td><td class="mono">31</td><td class="mono">16</td><td class="mono">237</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arpaia</td><td class="mono">22</td><td class="mono">259</td><td class="mono">63</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arienzo</td><td class="mono">0 — not indexed</td><td class="mono">—</td><td class="mono">—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>Paolisi holds the largest Falco cluster in the valley</strong> — and it is where Francesco
  Falco came from when he turned up at Arienzo in 1714.</p>

  <h3>And a caution that matters more than the counts</h3>
  <p>716 Falco records at Paolisi and Moiano is a great many, and the temptation is to absorb them. The
  in-law names say don't. The Arienzo Falco marry <strong>Crisci</strong> (113 register pages),
  <strong>Morgillo</strong> (76), <strong>Martone</strong> (49), <strong>de Lucia</strong> (40). Set
  that against the others:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>Who the Falco there married</th><th>Shared with Arienzo</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Paolisi</strong></td><td class="small">Carbone, Maietta, Lanni, De Gregorio, Nola, Maddaloni, Perrotta, Guerriero, Mauro, Girardi, Tirone, Cioffi</td><td><strong>Ruggiero, Rivetti</strong> only</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Moiano</strong></td><td class="small">Amoriello, Maglione, Tedesco, Massaro, Cesare, Tirino, Buonanno, Palma, Mauriello, Izzo, Ciaramella, Iodice</td><td><strong>Morgillo</strong> only</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>These look like <strong>largely different populations</strong> — which is exactly what this
  archive's founding premise predicts, since <em>Falco</em> was coined independently many times over.
  <span class="chip">caveat</span> the comparison sets 1861–1942 civil records against 1650–1830 parish
  records, so some divergence follows from the dates alone. Suggestive, not conclusive — and not a
  reason to merge anyone into the line.</p>
  <p class="small">One further negative worth recording: across all 1,042 indexed Zampiello and
  Annecchino records there is <strong>no Zampiello–Annecchino marriage</strong>. They shared four towns
  for a century without marrying each other.</p>

  <h2>The processetti — and one volume that matters</h2>
  <p><strong>321 volumes</strong> of marriage supplements: Arienzo 63, Forchia 73, Arpaia 43,
  Moiano 99, Paolisi 43. Three hard constraints came out of the reconnaissance.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>They stop at 1865.</strong> Forchia and Arpaia return nothing for 1866–1950 — so the
    marriages of Carminantonio and Filomena in the 1920s, and of Angelo Zampiello and Giuseppa Ferrara,
    are not there. Nor are the marriage registers for those years.</li>
    <li><strong>Arienzo is not name-indexed at all</strong> — zero records for Falco or Crisci. The
    home parish can only be read as images.</li>
    <li><strong>The images are Cloudflare-blocked to direct fetch.</strong> The IIIF manifest is open
    and gives every page URL, but those URLs return a block; they render only inside the viewer on the
    record page.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>The target is identified.</strong> Vincenzo Falco married Andreana Crisci about 1817, and
  the Arienzo processetti for <strong>1817</strong> survive — ark <span class="mono">an_ua14158</span>,
  shelf-mark 66.1021, <strong>310 pages</strong>. Under Neapolitan practice a marriage file attaches the
  groom's birth extract, the parents' consents, and <strong>the death certificates of any parent or
  paternal grandparent already dead</strong>.</p>
  <p>If Matteo Falco's death act survives anywhere, that volume is where it will be — and it would name
  <strong>Matteo's own father</strong>, which is the question this archive has never been able to
  answer. Reading it means paging a viewer through 310 images. Slow, but it is the obvious next piece
  of work.</p>


  <h2>Read from the images: the marriage act of 1814</h2>
  <p>Antenati's images are Cloudflare-blocked to direct download, but they render inside a page served
  from Antenati's own domain, and IIIF's region syntax crops and magnifies them server-side. That made
  the handwriting legible, and it produced a document.</p>
  <blockquote>
    <strong>Numero d'ordine 26.</strong> L'anno mille ottocento <strong>quattordici</strong>, a dì
    <strong>quindici 15</strong> del mese di <strong>Dicembre</strong>… è comparso
    <strong>Vincenzo Falco</strong>, di anni <strong>ventuno</strong>, di professione
    <strong>contadino</strong>… <strong>figlio maggiore di Pasquale Falco</strong>… e comparsa
    egualmente <strong>Andreana [Crisci]</strong>… <strong>figlia di Arcangelo</strong>.
    <span class="small">— Arienzo, civil marriages 1814, act 26</span>
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The date was wrong and is now right.</strong> This archive had the marriage at about
    1817, inferred from where it sat in the parish register. The civil act fixes it:
    <strong>15 December 1814</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Vincenzo was 21</strong> — born 1793, confirming the family record from an independent
    source.</li>
    <li><strong>He was a <em>contadino</em></strong>, a peasant farmer. The first occupation recorded
    for that generation.</li>
    <li><strong>Pasquale was alive.</strong> "Figlio maggiore di Pasquale Falco" — eldest son, and the
    father named without <em>fu</em>, "the late". So Pasquale Falco was living in December 1814.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>That last point closes off a hope. Because the father was alive, the marriage file would have
  carried <strong>Pasquale's consent</strong> — not the death certificates of dead ancestors. Matteo
  Falco's death act was never going to be attached to this marriage.</p>

  <h3>And the file itself is missing</h3>
  <p>The 1814 processetti volume was worked from the back. Act <strong>25</strong> is there — the
  publication for Francesco Crisci and Teresa Morgillo, 20 November 1814, with the archpriest's
  certificate behind it. Then comes a divider card reading
  <em>"Anno 1815 · Arienzo (66) · dal n° 1 al n° 25"</em>, and then January 1815.</p>
  <p><strong>The volume runs to act 25 and stops.</strong> The second 1814 volume is a separate short
  series starting again at n° 1. <strong>Act 26's supporting file is in neither.</strong> Whether it was
  never filed, was lost, or sits in a volume nobody has scanned is not established — only that it is not
  on Antenati.</p>


  <h2>Hunting Pasquale's death — and finding his wife's</h2>
  <p>With Matteo unreachable through the marriage file, the better route above him was
  <strong>Pasquale Falco's own death act</strong>: a civil death record names the deceased's parents
  outright. The hunt turned up his wife's instead, and it was worth more than expected.</p>
  <blockquote>
    …die vero decima sexta 16: <strong>Clara Rivetti</strong>, <em>uxor quondam <strong>Paschalis
    Falco</strong></em>, <em>filia quondam <strong>Nicolai</strong> et <strong>Anna</strong></em>…,
    aetatis suae annorum <strong>octoginta 80</strong>… obiit, et in Sodalitate Sanctae Mariae
    Gratiarum eius cadaver humatum est.
    <span class="small">— Arienzo parish deaths, 1843</span>
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A generation nobody had.</strong> Chiara Rivetti was the daughter of the late
    <strong>Nicola Rivetti</strong> and <strong>Anna</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>She was already a widow</strong>, so Pasquale was dead by September 1843. Her stated age
    of 80 puts her birth near 1763; the family record says 1772, so one of the two is loose. Ages in
    death records are routinely approximate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>That bracketed his death to 1829–1843</strong> — named without <em>fu</em> at
  his son's marriage in December 1814, named again without <em>quondam</em> in his daughter Angela's
  death entry of 1829, and dead by 1843. The Arienzo civil death registers survive one volume per year
  across that window, all digitised, all without an alphabetical index. So they were read linearly:
  1835 first, then 1836, then 1834.</p>

  <h2>Found: 1 October 1834</h2>
  <p>Arienzo, <span class="mono">an_ua14336</span>, image 43 of 57, page 236, <strong>act no. 69</strong>.</p>
  <blockquote>
    …sono comparsi <strong>Virgilio Saccavino</strong>, di anni quarantacinque, di professione
    contadino, domiciliato ivi, <strong>strada Camellara</strong>, vicino del defunto, e
    <strong>Nicola Corsi</strong>, di anni trenta… i quali han dichiarato, che nel giorno
    <strong>primo</strong> del mese di <strong>Ottobre</strong>, anno corrente, alle ore
    <strong>cinque</strong>, è morto nella sua propria casa <strong>Pasquale Falco</strong>, d'anni
    settantaquattro, <em>marito di <strong>Chiara Rivetti</strong></em>, di professione
    <strong>contadino</strong>, nato in Arienzo, <em>figlio di <strong>Matteo Falco, defunto</strong>,
    di professione contadino, e di <strong>Francesca Crisci, defunta</strong></em>.
    <span class="small">— Arienzo, Stato civile della restaurazione, Morti 1834, atto 69</span>
  </blockquote>
  <figure style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <img src={u("/plates/pasquale-falco-death-1834.jpg")} alt="Act 69, Arienzo civil death register 1834" style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid #ccc" />
    <figcaption class="small">Page 236 of the register. Archivio di Stato di Caserta, via Antenati.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The oldest open question in the line is closed.</strong> He died at five in the morning
    at his own house on strada Camellara, and was buried from there.</li>
    <li><strong>The parentage is confirmed twice over.</strong> The 1767 baptism gave Matteo Falco and
    Francesca Crisci from the parish register; this gives them again from the civil register, sixty-seven
    years later, from two neighbours rather than a priest. Two institutions, two record series, one answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Both of his parents were already dead</strong> by October 1834 — each marked
    <em>defunto</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Chiara Rivetti was still alive.</strong> He is recorded as her husband, not she as his
    widow. She outlived him by nine years.</li>
    <li><strong>The age does not fit.</strong> Seventy-four implies a birth around 1760; the baptism is
    October 1767, which would make him sixty-six. The declarants were neighbours, not family. The
    parentage and the wife identify him; the age is the loose figure, and is recorded here as such.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Three more Falco deaths in the same year</h2>
  <p>Reading the volume in full — rather than stopping at the target — turned up three others, and
  together they put an address on the family.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pasquale Falco, ten days old</strong>, died 25 December 1834, son of
    <strong>Vincenzo Falco</strong> of strada Camellara. Vincenzo is the elder Pasquale's eldest son —
    named as such in his own marriage act of 15 December 1814. This child was born about a fortnight
    before he died, eleven weeks after his grandfather, and given his name. He is the ancestor's
    namesake and his grandson, and he lived ten days.</li>
    <li><strong>Maria Rosa Falco, sixteen</strong>, <em>contadina</em>, died 30 May 1834, daughter of
    <strong>Michele Falco</strong> of strada Camellara. Michele's household buried a fourteen-month-old
    son, Salvatore, in August 1836.</li>
    <li><strong>Anna Falco, eight days old</strong>, died 7 March 1834, daughter of
    <strong>Michelangelo Falco</strong> of strada Camellara — a name new to this archive.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Going back a year further: 1833</h2>
  <p>With the target found, the neighbouring years are still worth reading — the Camellara cluster is
  unexplained, and every act names parents. 1833 (<span class="mono">an_ua14335</span>, 58 images)
  yielded one Falco, and it turned out to matter.</p>
  <blockquote>
    Num. d'ordine <strong>sedici</strong>… sono comparsi <strong>Grazio Torrino</strong>, di anni
    quaranta, di professione contadino, domiciliato <strong>strada Camellara</strong>, vicino del
    defunto, ed <strong>Angelo Lombardo</strong>, di anni trenta… i quali han dichiarato, che nel
    giorno <strong>ventidue</strong> del mese di <strong>Febrajo</strong>, anno corrente,
    <strong>alle ore una di notte</strong>, è morto nella sua propria casa
    <strong>Pasquale Falco, di anni tre</strong>, nato in Arienzo, <em>figlio di
    <strong>Vincenzo Falco</strong>, di professione contadino, e di
    <strong>Andreana Crisci</strong></em>.
    <span class="small">— Arienzo, Morti 1833, atto 16</span>
  </blockquote>
  <figure style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <img src={u("/plates/pasquale-falco-child-death-1833.jpg")} alt="Act 16, Arienzo civil death register 1833" style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid #ccc" />
    <figcaption class="small">Act no. 16, 22 February 1833. Archivio di Stato di Caserta, via Antenati.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The first civil record of Vincenzo Falco and Andreana Crisci as a household</strong>,
    at their address. Until this, the couple was attested only by their marriage act of December 1814
    and by their son Raffaele's baptism in 1818.</li>
    <li><strong>He used his father's name twice, and buried the child both times.</strong> This
    Pasquale died at one in the morning on 22 February 1833, aged three, while his grandfather was
    still alive. The grandfather died on 1 October 1834. About a fortnight after that, Vincenzo's next
    son was born and given the name again — and died at ten days old on Christmas Day. Two children,
    one name, twenty-two months apart.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why these years cannot be searched, only read</h2>
  <p>Each annual volume has a printed form bound into the back: <em>“Tavola alfabetica annuale de’
  Morti per l’anno mille ottocento…”</em> — an alphabetical index, with ruled columns for surname,
  forename, birthplace, profession, parents and date of death. In Arienzo <strong>it was left
  blank</strong>. The clerk signed the closing certificate and never filled the table in.</p>
  <p>That is the concrete reason the 1829–1843 hunt could not be shortcut, and it is worth stating
  plainly for anyone who comes after: it is not that these volumes lack an index. It is that Arienzo's
  index pages were never completed. There is no substitute for reading the acts.</p>

  <h2>Strada Camellara</h2>
  <p>In this one year, five Falco householders are recorded on the same street: <strong>Pasquale</strong>,
  his son <strong>Vincenzo</strong>, <strong>Michele</strong>, <strong>Michelangelo</strong>, and — from
  the 1836 volume — <strong>Francesco</strong>. Every one of them a <em>contadino</em>, and the
  declarants who came to the town hall for each death were neighbours from the same street. Whether the
  four besides Vincenzo are Pasquale's other sons, his brothers' sons, or unrelated Falco is not yet
  established — but they are one neighbourhood, and the answer is in the birth registers.</p>
  <p>The gentry Falco of Arienzo — Don Francesco Falco, <em>proprietario</em>, and his daughter
  Donna Raffaela, a nun of the Ave Gratia Plena, both in the 1836 register — live elsewhere in the town.
  Nothing so far joins them to this family, and this archive does not join them.</p>

  <p class="small"><strong>Where it was searched for and not found</strong>, so nobody repeats it: 1,145
  pages of the Arienzo parish death register harvested from FamilySearch's full text — searched by name,
  by OCR variant, and by male-adult-with-age across 1828–1845; and every one of the 1,162 Falco records
  in FamilySearch's index of Caserta civil registration 1809–1866, of which 249 fall in the window. The
  only "Pasquale Falco" principals there are two <em>Falcone</em> men of other families. The act exists
  in none of them. It was found only by reading the register itself, act by act — 1835 and 1836 in full
  and empty of him, then 1834.</p>

  <h2>What this changes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Antenati is <strong>reachable</strong>. Earlier work recorded it as blocked; it is not, and the
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>open questions</a> have been updated accordingly.</li>
    <li>Two unproven births are now <strong>located rather than merely missing</strong> — a specific
    volume each, in a specific archive.</li>
    <li><strong>Moiano and Paolisi</strong> join the list of places this family has to be searched in.</li>
    <li>The <em>processetti</em> are the largest unworked seam — 321 volumes — but they stop at 1865,
    which rules them out for the twentieth-century marriages.</li>
    <li><strong>Pasquale Falco's death act has been found</strong>, and with it his parentage
    confirmed from a second, independent record series. The 1829–1843 search is closed.</li>
    <li><strong>Strada Camellara</strong> is the family's address, and four more Falco householders
    on it are now named.</li>
    <li><strong>Four annual death registers have been read in full</strong> — 1833, 1834, 1835 and
    1836 — and the page-by-page logs are published with the archive, so the reading does not have to
    be repeated. Eight Falco deaths in four years, all but one of them on the same street.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
